board_attiny3217 — modern-AVR (tinyAVR 1-series) blink demo
===========================================================

The tinyAVR-1 sibling of examples/board_atmega4809. It exercises the same
modern engine and peripheral models, on the other modern family this fork adds:

  * avr_port_modern  — PA0 driven via the PORT register block (DIRSET/OUTTGL)
  * avr_usart_modern — USART0 greeting + counter on the default TXD pin (PB2)

The firmware (attiny3217_blink.c) is written against the device header's
memory-mapped peripheral structs (PORTA, PORTB, USART0), not the flat
<avr/io.h> register macros used by the classic examples.

Build & run (from this folder)
------------------------------

Open a console, change into this folder, build, then run:

    cd examples/board_attiny3217     # the demo loads its .axf from here
    make                             # builds the host program + firmware (.axf)
    ./obj-*/blink.elf                # run the demo

The runnable program is the host binary built under obj-<arch>/ (for example
obj-x86_64-pc-linux-gnu/blink.elf); the ./obj-*/ glob just picks it regardless
of architecture. Run it from this folder so it finds attiny3217_blink.axf.

Expected output: the firmware's USART0 greeting and "tick N" counter echoed to
the terminal (the host hooks USART0's UART IRQ), interleaved with the first few
PA0 toggles, then a summary — PA0 toggled 20 times over 200 simulated ms — plus
a GTKWave-openable trace written to attiny3217_blink.vcd.

Toolchain note
--------------

Building the firmware needs a modern avr-gcc (12+) that ships the tinyAVR-1
device headers (verified with avr-gcc 16) — the same requirement as running any
firmware on this fork. The Makefile detects whether avr-gcc can target
attiny3217 and, if not, skips the firmware step (so a plain `make` across
examples/ still succeeds) while still building the host binary. Install a modern
avr-gcc and re-run `make` to build attiny3217_blink.axf and run the demo.

Want every supported modern part, not just this one?
-----------------------------------------------------

See examples/board_modern_avr, which builds one blink firmware for every
supported modern core (all 15 tinyAVR-1 + 8 megaAVR-0) and runs each, reporting
which boot and blink.
